WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.649 --> 00:00:10.199
In 1993 Steven Spielberg directed Schindler's List: the film he considers the most important of his career. The movie shows his

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:16.289
mastery of visual imagery. Perhaps the most famous symbol in this movie is the little girl in the red coat wandering aimlessly

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:22.289
during the liquidation of the Krakow ghetto. Spielberg said she symbolizes how obvious the Holocaust was, yet

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:23.880
nobody did anything to stop it.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.890
It was almost as obvious as a little girl wearing a red coat walking down the street. When he talks about the scene,

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:34.709
he mentions the impact of having a color show up in the middle of a black-and-white movie, but he doesn't mention the color itself,

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:35.739
which begs the question:

00:00:35.739 --> 00:00:41.909
why does red work so well in the scene? If all that matters is having color show up in black and white, the symbolism would

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.350
be the same if her coat was blue.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.110
But blue

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:49.860
drastically changes the feeling of the scene, as would any other color. Each color is packed with its own symbolism.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:55.679
Filmmakers may not know all the nuances of a colors history and meaning, but they understand each color's emotional payoff.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:02.039
So what symbolism is unique to the color red? Why do some filmmakers make such pointed, careful use of red?

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.879
The color definitely has a very unusual

00:01:06.729 --> 00:01:12.929
biological effect on humans. It evokes all sorts of passionate emotions: when we feel angry, embarrassed, or flirtatious,

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.799
we literally get red in the face. If two boxers of equal skill fight each other,

00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:22.649
the one in red is actually more likely to win. Several studies suggest that it's the color people look most attractive wearing.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:28.650
There's even an urban legend that red cars are more likely to get pulled over. Red stands out amongst other colors, and

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.079
filmmakers have always understood this phenomenon.

00:01:31.079 --> 00:01:34.829
The screenwriters of The Wizard of Oz wanted to show off the beauty of Technicolor,

00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:40.259
so they had to change the plain silver slippers from the novel. The sought-after and powerful slippers did not become

00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:43.319
emerald green or bright yellow: one color suited them best.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:48.689
If something is important, red gets the job done on its own. We use the color red to

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:57.239
indicate anything in the real world that has been tainted by the other world. And red is used specifically to connote

00:01:57.759 --> 00:01:58.900
really

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.900
explosively emotional

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.879
moments and situations. It's no surprise that red is mankind's original color.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.699
Not only was it the first color used in art and cave paintings,

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:14.880
but nearly every Western language has a name for it before any other color. Before Newton's rainbow, early philosophers

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:19.139
put red in the middle of the color spectrum directly in between black and white.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:25.919
So it's no surprise that many filmmakers have an instinct to place black white and red together. It seems like reducing the color spectrum

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:33.089
exponentially enhances the symbolic weight of these three colors: Snow White, the Evil Queen dressed in Black, the Red Apple.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:38.640
But I think to really get at the meaning of such a unique and important color, and why a scene like in Schindler's List would

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.739
use it, we have to look at its historical significance.

00:02:41.049 --> 00:02:43.889
The perception of color is not just a biological phenomenon

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:51.030
but a cultural one, and it's symbolic power comes from two objects: fire and blood, two elements always associated with red and

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:56.880
universally encountered by every society throughout history. Even today whenever a dictionary defines red,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.189
they typically use fire and blood in their explanation. The relationship between red and blood makes sense,

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:09.570
since all living vertebrates have red blood. But the association of red with fire is less clear, after all, fire isn't exactly red

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:14.879
but more of an orange or yellow, why do we culturally link red with the color of fire? Historians argue

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.699
it's because many Asian societies saw fire as a living being. It's domestication was the symbolic birth of

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:27.239
civilization and like the Sun, it is a source of life by providing light and heat. Blood and fire were always at the center of

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:34.320
early religious traditions. Animal blood sacrifices were thought to lead to good harvests and many believed fire was passed down from the gods,

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.319
like when Prometheus stole it in Greek mythology. A key

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:43.679
component of these two elements is their symbolism of both life and death. On one hand, fire keeps you warm, protects you from

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:48.419
predators and lights up your home, but once it's out of control, fire leads to death and destruction.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:50.969
Similarly blood is a source of human life,

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:57.660
but the sight of blood indicates injury and too much means death. Christian stories clearly reflect our ancient contradictory

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:03.200
relationships with fire and blood. On one hand, God uses fire to communicate with Moses through a burning bush,

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.940
but fire also symbolizes the eternal tormenting of hell, explaining why hell is always red.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:12.340
Blood does represent Jesus's gruesome crucifixion, but Cardinals

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:19.749
re-appropriated that meaning into something positive by wearing red as a symbol of Jesus's blood sacrifice. Red symbolism varies wildly

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.480
depending on the time and place, but it always ties back to these core elements. Red is a symbol of love and passion

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:29.649
but it also became a symbol of prostitution and illicit sex in the Middle Ages

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:33.339
showing the pitfalls of too much passion, of getting too close to the flame,

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:39.010
so to speak. Red was the symbol of the French Revolution, and from then on the color became a sign of extreme political

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:43.300
upheaval; oftentimes violent, always passionate, aggressive, and filled with life.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:48.219
The point is if a critic looks at red through the lens of its origins in fire and blood, then the

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:55.480
symbolic power of red becomes much more obvious. Many movies use red to tap into these powerful historical roots. Red is heat,

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.119
it is a symbol of life and light in an otherwise

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:00.610
desolate and cold world. It adorns

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:06.819
energetic youthful characters. Wes Anderson loves it because it injects visual life into his scenes. Red often

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:14.589
sexualizes female characters, echoing its association with love and lust, but sometimes red goes too far. [Morpheus] Are you listening to me Neo?

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:18.760
Or were you looking at the woman in the red dress? [Neo] I was- [Morpheus] Look again.

00:05:22.379 --> 00:05:29.779
As much as red indicates life, it just as easily signals death and horror. What was one's passion becomes obsession to the point of self-destruction.

00:05:30.419 --> 00:05:34.698
No color better captures youth and sexuality, and the boys of a Clockwork Orange

00:05:34.979 --> 00:05:37.639
represent this sexuality to a dangerous extreme.

00:05:37.639 --> 00:05:44.269
Sometimes a red car is just an easy way to indicate speed, but it sometimes goes deeper than that. Cameron's dad loves his red car

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:46.460
to the extreme, more so than even his own son.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.359
The car in Boyz n the Hood is a looming sign of death that appears out of nowhere

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.299
the idea that there is no escape for these characters gets

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:59.630
reinforced in the opening shot, an example of red as the colour of warning and danger. Movies also channel their religious significance

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:04.339
of the color. In Mean Streets, Charlie holds his hand over flames in a form of personal

00:06:04.409 --> 00:06:06.949
penance, a nod to his fear of the flames of hell.

00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:14.568
It's no coincidence that Tony's bar is bathed in red: an indication that the place contains sex, blood, and violence; a world Charlie wants

00:06:14.569 --> 00:06:18.019
to escape. Perhaps the most powerful use of red is when filmmakers can

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:24.829
juxtapose its positive and negative symbolism, showing the two kinds of red side by side. The ending of Don't Look Now has a bizarre

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.520
montage revealing the true

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:33.109
identity of the girl in the red coat. Part of the horror of the finale is the contrast of different classic red images: the youthful

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:38.479
coat followed by his blood, then sex, and even flames in a church. All these images mixed together

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.749
disorients the viewer through the unusual pairings of red.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:50.000
Schindler's List perfectly embodies all the symbolic uses of red: its origins in fire and blood, the religious significance

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:52.519
developed from pagan rituals all the way to

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:58.669
Christianity, and the harnessing of the color's dual meanings. The movie begins with the lighting of a flame as a Jewish family

00:06:58.889 --> 00:07:03.769
celebrates the Sabbath. Color fades away when the fire goes out, and the story descends into Cracow Poland

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:09.650
during World War II. Right from the beginning the film establishes fire as a symbol of Jewish tradition and to the

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:15.769
extinguishing of the flame foreshadows the end of Jewish life in Poland. The recurring flames throughout the movie symbolize both

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:18.109
danger and life. In one scene, Spielberg

00:07:18.419 --> 00:07:25.908
superimposes a burning flame onto Oscar Schindler's chest to symbolize his gradual transformation, from a greedy businessman to a benevolent savior.

00:07:25.908 --> 00:07:32.140
The flames of his factory are a source of life as he saves Jewish people from extermination, in contrast to the flames

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:38.360
from the Płaszów labor camp burn bright and harsh in the foreground, as the Nazis walk into the factory in the background; a

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:44.680
reminder of the death and destruction they bring with them. At their most extreme flames are associated with horrific mass graves,

00:07:44.979 --> 00:07:49.058
but when Schindler invites his Jewish workers to practice the Sabbath, the flames become color

00:07:49.059 --> 00:07:50.860
once again. In Schindler's Factory

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:55.059
the Jewish tradition can continue: he provides light in a world surrounded by darkness.

00:07:55.269 --> 00:07:56.869
Flames are at the same time a

00:07:56.869 --> 00:08:01.629
symbol of the Jewish people surviving, and a symbol of their destruction. In this movie blood and death

00:08:01.629 --> 00:08:07.088
also go hand in hand with numerous executions and murders. But one scene uses blood in a more subtle,

00:08:07.089 --> 00:08:12.969
but equally disturbing way. When the Nazis want to separate the healthy from the sick, a group of women in the camp apply their own

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:16.838
blood as makeup. They're using blood to try to appear more alive.

00:08:16.839 --> 00:08:22.659
It's such a cruel irony that they have to bleed to look more lively, so they can in turn avoid a bloody death,

00:08:22.909 --> 00:08:27.998
self-harm is a form of self-preservation. In comparison to the rest of the movie this moment may seem benign,

00:08:27.999 --> 00:08:32.919
but it perfectly captures the absurdity of the Holocaust, how life and death were so disturbingly

00:08:33.079 --> 00:08:35.979
intertwined; blood is death and life all at once.

00:08:35.979 --> 00:08:39.818
The red coat is an incredible summation of all these ideas: on one hand

00:08:39.819 --> 00:08:42.938
the little girl is youth, life, and innocence. The red calls

00:08:43.009 --> 00:08:46.929
attention to her fiery warmth and young blood, but once she hides under the bed,

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:51.430
her red coat loses its color and we see it later in a whole new deadly context.

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:55.299
The coat is now presumably red with blood, to be disposed of in flames.

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:59.019
Schindler's List and the girl in the coat are the story of the color red.

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:01.869
They are a story of death and life coexisting together.

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:07.179
The red of Oskar Shindler is the red of life surrounded by his red is the red of the Holocaust

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:11.360
"The list... is life.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.660
All around its margins lies the gulf."

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:21.100
Not every instance of the color is as extreme as Schindler's List

00:09:21.119 --> 00:09:24.689
but in overt uses of red the same symbolic principles apply.

00:09:24.689 --> 00:09:29.908
It has a powerful and primal response for the viewer, coming from the human understanding of blood and fire.

00:09:30.159 --> 00:09:36.989
Filmmakers don't necessarily have knowledge of the actual symbolic history of different colors, but the best ones definitely know how to use it.

00:09:37.869 --> 00:09:41.459
Long before directing, artists understood the strengths of different colors.

00:09:41.459 --> 00:09:47.368
If a Calvinist painter wanted to give prominence to someone in their painting all they had to do was add a red sash. Any artist

00:09:47.369 --> 00:09:50.249
can benefit from knowing the various strengths and weaknesses of each color.

